# larch-os package list — installed via debootstrap second-stage
# Lines starting with # are comments; blank lines are ignored.
# Processed by scripts/setup-chroot.sh

# ── Base / boot ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
linux-image-amd64
systemd
systemd-sysv
udev
dbus
dbus-user-session
libpam-systemd
busybox-static
bash
coreutils
util-linux
procps
findutils
grep
sed
gawk
tar
gzip
bzip2
xz-utils
zstd
iproute2
iputils-ping
less
file
which
mount
kmod
pciutils
usbutils
dmidecode
lsof
strace
vim-tiny
nano
curl
wget
ca-certificates
openssl
login
passwd
# Dictionary for libpwquality — without it GNOME Settings rejects every password
cracklib-runtime
adduser
locales

# ── Graphics / Wayland ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
xwayland
mesa-vulkan-drivers
libgl1-mesa-dri
libgles2
libdrm2
libgbm1
libegl1
libwayland-client0
libwayland-server0

# ── GNOME desktop ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gnome-shell
gdm3
gnome-session
gnome-settings-daemon
gnome-keyring
gnome-control-center
gnome-backgrounds
gnome-themes-extra
adwaita-icon-theme
nautilus
gnome-terminal
gvfs
gvfs-backends
gvfs-fuse
# No image viewer / PDF viewer / text editor in the core image — the core
# desktop is Nautilus + GNOME Terminal only. Users get Loupe, Papers, Text
# Editor etc. from Flathub.

# ── Boot splash (OEM/firmware logo via BGRT) ─────────────────────────────────
# plymouth shows a graphical splash; the bgrt theme reuses the firmware's
# ACPI BGRT logo (the manufacturer logo) so boot continues that image instead
# of scrolling logs. Started from systemd post-switch_root (sysinit.target).
plymouth
plymouth-themes

# ── Flatpak / portals ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
flatpak
xdg-desktop-portal
xdg-desktop-portal-gnome
xdg-utils
xdg-user-dirs

# ── Audio ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
pipewire
pipewire-pulse
pipewire-alsa
wireplumber
libspa-0.2-bluetooth
libspa-0.2-jack

# ── Networking ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
network-manager
libnm0
wpasupplicant
wireless-tools
iw

# ── Time ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# systemd-timesyncd: SNTP client (Debian's NTP pool via FallbackNTP=), enabled by
# default. It also makes timedated report CanNTP, which GNOME Settings' "Automatic
# Date & Time" switch needs. geoclue-2.0: the location service gsd-datetime uses
# for "Automatic Time Zone" (GNOME Shell is its agent); on by default via
# rootfs-overlay/usr/share/glib-2.0/schemas/90_larch.gschema.override.
systemd-timesyncd
geoclue-2.0

# ── Bluetooth ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
bluez

# ── Printing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# cups: full CUPS metapackage (daemon + client + common). printer-driver-all:
# broad driver coverage (gutenprint, foomatic, hpijs, splix, ...) matching the
# "broad hardware coverage" approach already used for WiFi firmware below.
# cups-pk-helper: polkit-mediated admin actions — what GNOME Settings' Printers
# panel uses to add/remove/configure queues without a root password (see the
# polkit rule in rootfs-overlay/etc/polkit-1/rules.d/99-larch-cups.rules).
# avahi-daemon: DNS-SD discovery so network/AirPrint printers show up in the
# "Add Printer" dialog. ipp-usb: driverless IPP-over-USB support for modern
# (2015+) USB printers. system-config-printer: gnome-control-center's printer
# panel talks to its D-Bus service (org.fedoraproject.Config.Printing) during
# add-printer to group duplicate discovered devices — without it, adding any
# printer fails outright (GDBus ServiceUnknown), even though the GUI itself is
# unused; only the daemon side of this package is needed.
cups
cups-pk-helper
printer-driver-all
avahi-daemon
ipp-usb
system-config-printer

# ── Fonts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
fonts-noto
fonts-noto-color-emoji
fonts-dejavu
fonts-liberation

# ── Installer build dependencies ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# These packages are not used by the installed larch OS at runtime. They are
# present so that stage 8 (build-installer) can copy their binaries and shared
# libraries into the installer initramfs: mkfs.fat (dosfstools), mkfs.ext4
# (e2fsprogs), sfdisk/wipefs/blockdev (util-linux — already in base above).
# systemd-boot provides the BOOTX64.EFI binary placed on the ISO in stage 9.
dosfstools
e2fsprogs
systemd-boot
# dialog drives the installer's full-screen configuration form (ncurses). Copied
# into the installer initramfs in stage 8; the installer falls back to plain text
# prompts if it (or a terminfo entry) is absent.
dialog

# ── Firmware (broad coverage for common hardware) ────────────────────────────
firmware-linux-free
firmware-linux-nonfree
firmware-iwlwifi
firmware-realtek
firmware-atheros
firmware-brcm80211
firmware-mediatek

# ── NVIDIA proprietary driver (Pascal and newer) ─────────────────────────────
# Nouveau cannot do 3D acceleration on Pascal+ GPUs (signed-firmware reclocking
# is unavailable), so e.g. a GTX 1070 Ti falls back to llvmpipe and GL apps fail
# with `nvc0_screen_create ... -19`. The proprietary driver supports Maxwell→Ada
# and runs fine on Wayland once nvidia-drm modeset is enabled — see the
# `nvidia-drm.modeset=1` kernel arg in bootloader/entries/larch.conf, which is
# also what makes GDM offer the Wayland session (61-gdm.rules disables Wayland
# when the NVIDIA driver is loaded without modeset). It builds its kernel module
# via nvidia-kernel-dkms at stage 4 (needs linux-headers-amd64, present below for
# broadcom) and ships its own nouveau blacklist in /etc/modprobe.d. On machines
# without an NVIDIA GPU the module simply never loads — harmless, same as
# broadcom-sta below. Mesa stays installed for those machines; GLVND dispatches
# to the right driver at runtime.
#
# NOT listed here: nvidia-driver is installed from trixie-backports by
# setup-chroot.sh, which adds the backports source and pins with
# `-t trixie-backports`. As of this writing backports carries 550.163.01 (same
# upstream version as main, just rebuilt for bpo13), so the pin is currently a
# no-op version-wise — it's kept forward-looking: when Debian backports a newer
# branch (e.g. 580.x, the last to support Pascal) a rebuild picks it up
# automatically. 550.x already supports Pascal (GTX 1070 Ti) fine.

# ── Broadcom BCM4360 WiFi (2015 MacBook Air) ─────────────────────────────────
# BCM4360 (14e4:43a0) has no open-source driver. broadcom-sta-dkms provides the
# proprietary wl.ko. linux-headers-amd64 is required for the DKMS build during
# stage 4 (also used by nvidia-kernel-dkms above); it is removed by
# clean-rootfs.sh after the modules are compiled.
broadcom-sta-dkms
linux-headers-amd64
